{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>The heart of the righteous meditates to answer, but the mouth of the wicked utters evil things (Proverbs 15:28).</b>" "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, taught in this verse (Proverbs 15) about the righteous and the wicked — how they are two opposites possessing two extremes, each man at his own end. He revealed to us the trait of lowliness that belongs to the righteous and the trait of pride that belongs to the wicked, so that a person will distance himself from the trait of pride and strengthen himself in the trait of lowliness. He said that the righteous man's heart meditates and thinks constantly, all day long, thoughts of submission and lowliness; and the word לענות is from (Exodus 10:3) לענות מפני, which the Targum renders להתכנעא — 'to submit.' And since this is his thought, surely his speech is likewise, for speech in a person reveals the inner chambers of thought. As the masters of ethics said: 'The tongue is the pen of the heart' — for since the hidden things of his inner chambers are good, they are revealed as their counterpart, for it is the messenger of his pen and testifies on his behalf. But behold, the wicked man is the reverse: not only does he think evil things in the inner chamber of his heart, words of pride and contempt, but יביע אותן בפיו — he pours them out from his mouth, so that they issue from his mouth and he publishes them, and he declares to every fool what he is. Thus the righteous man is the inverse, in what is hidden and in what is revealed.\n\nNow it is well known that lowliness is a supreme trait and an exalted quality, as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: the world endures only through one who makes himself like nothing, as it is said (Deuteronomy 33:27): ״ומתחת זרועות עולם״. This is the way of the righteous — they humble themselves: thus Abraham said (Genesis 18:27): ״ואנכי עפר ואפר״, and of Moses and Aaron it is written (Exodus 16): ״ונחנו מה״, and David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 22:7): ״ואנכי תולעת ולא איש״, and so forth. And the Shekhinah dwells only among the lowly of spirit, as it is written (Isaiah 57:15): ״מרום וקדוש אשכון ואת דכא ושפל רוח״. And the Torah endures only among the lowly of spirit, as the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: Why was the Torah likened to water, as it is said (ibid. 55) ״הוי כל צמא לכו למים״? Just as water leaves a high place and flows to a low place, so the Torah does not endure in one whose spirit is high, but in one whose mind is lowly. And so they said in the Midrash (Deuteronomy 30): ״לא בשמים היא״ — it is not found among the haughty of spirit, those who exalt themselves up to the heavens, but rather in one who kills himself over it, as it is said (Numbers 19:14): ״אדם כי ימות באהל״.\n\nAnd so we find regarding the Shekhinah: even though the principal abode of its dwelling and the seat of its rest is in the heavens, the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded that the principal dwelling of the Shekhinah was among the lower beings, as it is said (Genesis 3:8): ״וישמעו את קול ה' אלהים מתהלך בגן לרוח היום״. And King David, peace be upon him, mentioned this by way of the secret (sod) and said (Psalms 104:17): ״חסידה ברושים ביתה״. And once Adam sinned, it withdrew; afterward it returned and rested upon Mount Sinai, which is the lowliest of mountains. Among all the tall mountains of the world the Holy One, blessed be He, did not choose to give the Torah except upon it, as it is said (Psalms 68:17): ״ההר חמד אלהים לשבתו״. And upon this mountain all the commandments in their entirety were given, and this is what is written in the perashah of Shemittah." "<b>And HaShem spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai, saying.</b> The Sages, of blessed memory, were roused to ask: what reason is there that 'on Mount Sinai' is mentioned here? It should have said only וידבר ה' אל משה לאמר, as is its manner in every other place in the Torah. From this they expounded in Torat Kohanim: what does the subject of Shemittah have to do with Mount Sinai? Were not all the commandments given from Sinai? Rather, just as Shemittah — its general principles, its particular details, and its fine distinctions were stated from Sinai, so too all the commandments in their entirety — their general principles, their particular details, and their fine distinctions were stated from Sinai." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote that the meaning of this beraita in Torat Kohanim — which said that Shemittah's general principles, particular details, and fine distinctions were stated from Sinai — is to say that the general principles of Shemittah were stated from Sinai, as it is written in the perashah of ואלה המשפטים (Exodus 23:11): ״והשביעית תשמטנה ונטשתה ואכלו אביוני עמך״ — and these are the laws of Shemittah in general. And here He returned and said that on Mount Sinai all its particulars were also stated, for indeed they are all mentioned in this perashah. At the end of the matter it is written (Leviticus 27:34): ״אלה המצות אשר צוה ה' את משה אל בני ישראל בהר סיני״ — to draw an analogy between all the commandments and the Shemittah mentioned here: all of them were likewise stated in general and in particular, and all from Sinai. So it appears to me, and this is a כפתור ופרח (a knob and a flower — an ornament). Thus far the language of the Rav.\n\nAnd according to the opinion of the Rav, of blessed memory, 'on Mount Sinai' would refer to the second tablets, for the commandment of Shemittah was stated in general terms in the first covenant, and afterward in the second covenant it was stated in its particulars, its fine distinctions, and its penalties." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say the reason that 'on Mount Sinai' was mentioned: because Israel heard the commandment of Shabbat from the mouth of the Almighty on Mount Sinai, and Shemittah and Yovel are commandments that follow after Shabbat; therefore it was necessary to clarify here that even Shemittah and Yovel were commanded on Mount Sinai, for there is one reason for all of them.\n\nAnd according to what they expounded in Torat Kohanim, 'on Mount Sinai' would extend both to what precedes it and to what follows it: to what precedes it — namely what is said ויקב בן האשה הישראלית, for the Name that he heard on Mount Sinai he separated and blasphemed; to what follows it — the matter of Shemittah and Yovel. And just as Shemittah and Yovel are from Sinai, so too all the commandments are from Sinai."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :register {:flags {4 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:29.929317880Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}